 ## Background
-Local illumination and shadowing in volume rendering originated with the need to add realism to direct volume rendering (DVR) techniques, which initially relied on simple transfer functions without shading. Early work by Max [Max 1995] established the distinction between local and global illumination in the context of volume rendering, noting that local methods are cheap but lack shadows, while global methods are expensive [S1, unofficial]. The Two-Pass Shadow Casting method by Kajiya and Von Herzen [Kajiya and Von Herzen] provided a foundational approach for computing shadows in participating media by separating illumination and gathering passes. Later developments, such as Light Propagation Volumes by Kaplanyan [Kaplanyan], extended these concepts to real-time indirect illumination. Modern implementations often combine these techniques with real-time ray marching and precomputed textures for performance [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
+Local illumination techniques in volume rendering have been a staple of Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) since its early development, primarily because global illumination methods were computationally prohibitive for real-time or interactive applications [S1, unofficial]. The foundational work by Max [Max 1995] and subsequent implementations in libraries like Visualization Library [S8, unofficial] established the standard practice of using local shading models (absorption, emission, and simple shadowing) to enhance the perception of 3D structure in scalar fields [S1, unofficial]. The distinction between local and global illumination remains a key design choice in volume rendering pipelines, with local methods dominating real-time applications such as medical imaging and voxel-based games [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
 
